[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65679,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prepubertal fibroma is a benign mesenchymal neoplasm, probably of fibroblastic differentiation, that presents as a poorly marginated, infiltrative proliferation of spindle cells in the vulvar region of (mostly) prepubertal girls.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65680,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8810/0 Fibroma NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65681,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EE6Y &amp; XH8E66 Other specified fibromatous disorders of skin and soft tissue &amp; Fibroma NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65682,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> childhood asymmetrical labium majus enlargement</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65683,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65684,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This lesion arises in the vulvar region, as either a submucosal or a subcutaneous lesion. The labia majora are the most common site {15577679; 16006794}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65685,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prepubertal vulvar fibroma presents as a unilateral painless mass, swelling, or enlargement in the vulvar region of prepubertal girls, appearing as an asymmetrical enlargement of one labium majus {15577679; 16006794}. Lesions are slow-growing, often enlarging over a period of months to 3&nbsp;years {15577679}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65686,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prepubertal vulvar fibroma is rare, and the literature probably includes cases reported as childhood asymmetrical labium majus enlargement {16006794} and prepubertal unilateral fibrous hyperplasia of the labium majus {17601903}. Most patients are affected between the ages of 3 and 13&nbsp;years {15577679; 16006794}. Occasionally, patients may develop lesions identical to prepubertal fibroma in adulthood, including after menopause {17405983}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65687,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65688,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are no known predisposing genetic or environmental risk factors. Normal karyotypes have been reported for 6&nbsp;cases that have undergone cytogenetic analysis {16006794; 17601903}. Some authors have suggested that lesions develop as asymmetrical physiological enlargement secondary to hormonal changes during prepuberty and early puberty {16006794; 17601903}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65689,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours range in size from 2.0 to 8.0&nbsp;cm and have a pinkish-grey to tan cut surface with a soft or rubbery texture {15577679; 16006794}. The stroma may be myxoid. Necrosis and haemorrhage are not seen.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65690,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prepubertal vulvar fibroma is poorly marginated and shows diffuse infiltrative growth through pre-existing vulvar connective tissue {15577679; 16006794}. Lesions mainly involve submucosa and dermis, and they can entrap adnexal structures {15577679}. Prepubertal vulvar fibroma shows a hypocellular and patternless growth of bland spindled cells set within a variably collagenous, oedematous, or occasionally myxoid stroma. Collagen fibres, varying in appearance from thick to finely wispy, are common. Uniform tumour cells are predominantly spindled or occasionally stellate and have ovoid nuclei with fine chromatin and inconspicuous nucleoli. The cytoplasm is poorly defined and typically palely amphophilic in appearance. Nuclear atypia, pleomorphism, and necrosis are absent. Mitotic activity is sparse. CD34 staining is typically multifocal to strong and diffuse {15577679}. Immunohistochemical stains for S100, SMA, and desmin are consistently negative {15577679; 16006794}. Staining for ER and PR has been variably reported as negative, focal, or positive {15577679; 16006794; 17601903}; these are not considered useful diagnostic markers.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65691,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65692,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65693,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> hypocellular, patternless, and diffusely infiltrative growth of bland spindle cells; collagenous, oedematous, or occasionally myxoid stroma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positivity for CD34.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65694,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65695,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 502,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These lesions are clinically benign, although at least 30% of patients may have local recurrence, particularly after incomplete excision {15577679; 16006794}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]